      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-sky-600 mb-2">$100 + $85</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Visa fee plus biometrics, per person</div>
      </div>
      <div class="bg-white border border-slate-200 p-6 rounded-2xl text-center">
        <div class="text-3xl font-black text-sky-600 mb-2">80%</div>
        <div class="text-sm text-slate-600 font-medium">Share of past applications finished within the posted time</div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <nav class="bg-slate-50 border border-slate-200 rounded-2xl p-6 mb-10" aria-label="Table of contents">
      <p class="font-bold text-slate-900 mb-3">In this guide</p>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-1 text-blue-700">
        <li><a href="#by-country" class="hover:underline">Visitor visa processing times by country</a></li>
        <li><a href="#meaning" class="hover:underline">What the processing time actually means</a></li>
        <li><a href="#fees" class="hover:underline">Visitor visa fees in November 2026</a></li>
        <li><a href="#visa-or-eta" class="hover:underline">Visitor visa or eTA: which one you need</a></li>
        <li><a href="#imm5257" class="hover:underline">How to apply: IMM 5257 and documents</a></li>
        <li><a href="#super-visa" class="hover:underline">Visitor visa vs super visa</a></li>
        <li><a href="#status" class="hover:underline">How to check your application status</a></li>
        <li><a href="#delays" class="hover:underline">Why applications get delayed or refused</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq" class="hover:underline">Frequently asked questions</a></li>
      </ol>
    </nav>

    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none">

      <p class="text-xl leading-relaxed">
        If a parent, sibling or friend wants to visit you in Vancouver, Surrey or Kelowna, the first question is usually how long the visa will take. The honest answer is that it depends heavily on where they apply from, and the number IRCC publishes is a look backward at recent files rather than a guarantee. This guide pulls the current figures straight from IRCC's processing-time data, explains how to read them, and covers the fee, the form, the eTA alternative and the reasons applications stall. It is general information, not legal advice.
      </p>

      <h2 id="by-country" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Canada Visitor Visa Processing Times by Country (November 2026)</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC publishes visitor visa processing times by the country you apply from, which is normally where you live. The figures below were read from the data behind IRCC's official processing-time tool, which IRCC lists as last updated <strong>November 4, 2026</strong>. We have added the super visa time for the same countries because many B.C. families compare the two.
      </p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Applying from</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Visitor visa (outside Canada)</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Super visa</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">India</td><td class="px-4 py-3">32 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">85 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Philippines</td><td class="px-4 py-3">24 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">109 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">China</td><td class="px-4 py-3">51 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">89 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Pakistan</td><td class="px-4 py-3">58 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">101 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Nigeria</td><td class="px-4 py-3">103 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">33 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Vietnam</td><td class="px-4 py-3">39 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">46 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Bangladesh</td><td class="px-4 py-3">31 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">53 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Iran</td><td class="px-4 py-3">76 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">231 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Mexico</td><td class="px-4 py-3">14 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">78 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">United States</td><td class="px-4 py-3">18 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">75 days</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">United Kingdom</td><td class="px-4 py-3">23 days</td><td class="px-4 py-3">104 days</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>
      <p class="text-sm text-gray-500">Source: IRCC processing-time data, last updated November 4, 2026, retrieved November 13, 2026. Times change weekly; check the official tool before you apply.</p>

      <p>
        A few things stand out. Applications from Mexico, the United States and the Philippines are currently among the fastest, while Nigeria and Iran are among the slowest. The United States, United Kingdom and Mexico rows matter mostly for people who live there but hold a passport that needs a visa, for example an Indian citizen working in California; U.S. citizens do not need a visa at all, and U.K. citizens use an eTA. The Nigeria row is not a typo: the published super visa time (33 days) is shorter than the visitor visa time (103 days), because each line comes from a different pool of applications.
      </p>
      <p>
        IRCC's data also lists a separate figure of 310 days for extending your stay as a visitor (a visitor record) from inside Canada, as of November 4, 2026. If your relative is already here and wants to stay longer, apply for the extension well before their current status ends.
      </p>

      <h2 id="meaning" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">What the Visitor Visa Processing Time Actually Means</h2>

      <p>
        IRCC says most of its processing times are historical. For temporary residence applications such as visitor visas, it looks at how long it took to process <strong>80% of applications</strong> over the past 8 to 16 weeks. So if the tool says 32 days, roughly eight in ten recent applications from that country were decided within 32 days, and two in ten took longer. IRCC states plainly that the times are "not a maximum or a guarantee."
      </p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>When the clock starts:</strong> the day IRCC receives your complete application. For online applications, that is the day you submit.</li>
        <li><strong>When it stops:</strong> when an officer makes a decision. Sending your passport for the visa to be placed in it happens after that.</li>
        <li><strong>Biometrics are included:</strong> IRCC says the processing time includes the time it takes to give your biometrics. A slow appointment booking therefore eats into, and can push past, the posted figure.</li>
        <li><strong>Updates:</strong> the figures change as backlogs grow or shrink, so a time you saw last month may be out of date.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2 id="fees" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Canada Visitor Visa Fees in November 2026</h2>

      <p>These amounts are from IRCC's fee list as of November 13, 2026, in Canadian dollars:</p>

      <div class="overflow-x-auto my-8">
        <table class="min-w-full border border-slate-200 rounded-lg overflow-hidden">
          <thead class="bg-slate-100">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Item</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3 text-left text-sm font-bold text-slate-700">Fee</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-slate-200">
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Visitor visa, including super visa (per person)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$100</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Visitor visa, family of 5 or more applying together</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$500</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Biometrics (per person)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$85</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Biometrics, family applying together (maximum)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$170</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Extend your stay as a visitor (per person)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$100</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Restore your status as a visitor</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$246.25</td></tr>
            <tr><td class="px-4 py-3 font-medium">Electronic Travel Authorization (eTA)</td><td class="px-4 py-3">$7</td></tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <p>
        For a single first-time applicant, that is $185 in total. Children under 14 and applicants over 79 are exempt from biometrics, and U.S. nationals do not give biometrics for temporary residence applications. Fees are paid by card at the end of the online application.
      </p>

      <h2 id="visa-or-eta" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Visitor Visa or eTA: Which One Do You Need?</h2>

      <p>Your passport decides this, not where you live:</p>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Visitor visa required:</strong> citizens of visa-required countries, including India, Nigeria, China, Pakistan, Vietnam, Bangladesh and Iran, need a visitor visa for any way of travelling to Canada, including by car, bus or cruise ship.</li>
        <li><strong>eTA required:</strong> citizens of visa-exempt countries, such as the United Kingdom, France, Japan and Australia, need an eTA to fly to or through a Canadian airport. It costs $7 and IRCC says most are approved within minutes. No eTA is needed if they arrive by land or sea.</li>
        <li><strong>Neither:</strong> U.S. citizens and U.S. lawful permanent residents (green card holders).</li>
        <li><strong>eTA instead of a visa, for some:</strong> citizens of certain visa-required countries, including Mexico, the Philippines, Brazil, Thailand, Indonesia and Malaysia, can apply for an eTA if they are flying, are coming for a temporary stay (normally up to six months), and have either held a Canadian visitor visa in the past 10 years or currently hold a valid U.S. nonimmigrant visa. Travel by land or sea still requires a visitor visa.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your relative qualifies for the eTA route, it is usually far quicker than waiting weeks for a TRV. Use IRCC's "Find out if you need a visa" tool to confirm before booking flights.
      </p>

      <h2 id="imm5257" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Apply: Form IMM 5257 and the Documents You Need</h2>

      <p>
        The official form is <strong>IMM 5257, Application for visitor visa (temporary resident visa)</strong>. Most applicants now apply online through the IRCC portal, the new version of the portal (for adults applying alone for the first time without a representative) or the IRCC secure account, where the IMM 5257 questions are built into the online form. Paper applications are only for people who cannot apply online, for example because of a disability, or who travel with a refugee, stateless or non-citizen travel document.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Typical documents</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>A copy of a valid passport. IRCC advises it should be valid for at least six months from your travel date.</li>
        <li>Proof of funds, such as recent bank statements, showing you can pay for the trip.</li>
        <li>A letter from your employer or other proof of a job, business, studies or property at home.</li>
        <li>Your travel history and a simple itinerary.</li>
        <li>For family visits, a letter of invitation from your relative in Canada and proof of your relationship.</li>
        <li>A letter authorizing a minor to travel, if a child is travelling without both parents.</li>
        <li>Anything your country's visa office checklist adds; the portal generates a personalized list.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">After you submit</h3>
      <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>You receive a biometrics instruction letter. Book a visa application centre appointment straight away; the letter states your deadline.</li>
        <li>An officer reviews the file and may ask for more documents, an interview, a medical exam or a police certificate.</li>
        <li>If approved, you get a letter asking you to send your passport. You cannot travel until the visa is placed in it, and you should send the same passport you applied with.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        A visitor visa can be issued for up to 10 years or until the passport or biometrics expire, whichever comes first, but the officer decides the actual expiry date and many visas are issued for less.
      </p>

      <h2 id="super-visa" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Visitor Visa vs Super Visa for Parents and Grandparents</h2>

      <p>
        Parents and grandparents of Canadian citizens, permanent residents and registered Indians can apply for a super visa instead of a regular visitor visa. A super visa lets them stay for up to five years at a time, with multiple entries over up to 10 years, while a regular visitor normally stays up to six months. The government fee starts at the same $100, but the super visa asks for more: proof of the host's income, medical insurance and a medical exam among the required documents. As the table above shows, it is also slower from most countries, often by two months or more. For a short trip, a regular visitor visa is usually the faster choice. Our <a href="/articles/super-visa-canada" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">super visa guide</a> covers the requirements in detail.
      </p>

      <h2 id="status" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">How to Check Your Visitor Visa Application Status</h2>

      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li><strong>Your online account:</strong> sign in to whichever account you applied through: the IRCC secure account (GCKey or Sign-In Partner) or one of the IRCC portals (email sign-in). Messages, biometrics letters and the decision all appear there.</li>
        <li><strong>Application status tracker:</strong> an optional separate account that can show a more detailed timeline. You need your unique client identifier (UCI) and application number.</li>
        <li><strong>Paper applications:</strong> link the application to an IRCC secure account to follow it online.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        If your application is well past the posted processing time for your country, you can then contact IRCC through its web form. Before that point, enquiries rarely change anything.
      </p>

      <h2 id="delays" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Why Visitor Visa Applications Get Delayed or Refused</h2>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Common causes of delay</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>Waiting days or weeks to book biometrics after the instruction letter arrives.</li>
        <li>Requests for more documents, an interview, a medical exam or a police certificate.</li>
        <li>Submitting a different passport from the one in the application.</li>
        <li>Peak seasons and visa offices with large backlogs, which is why the same visa can take 14 days from one country and over 100 from another.</li>
      </ul>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Common reasons for refusal</h3>
      <p>
        IRCC's eligibility rules say a visitor must have a valid travel document, be in good health, have no criminal or immigration-related convictions, have enough money for the stay, and convince an officer that ties such as a job, home, financial assets or family will take them home at the end of the visit. Refusals usually trace back to one of those points: the officer was not convinced the person would leave, the funds looked thin or unexplained, the purpose of the trip was vague, or documents were missing. An incomplete application can be refused outright. The refusal letter explains the reason, so read it before you reapply, and only reapply when you can address it with new evidence.
      </p>

      <h3 class="text-2xl font-semibold mt-8 mb-4">Practical tips for B.C. hosts and their visitors</h3>
      <ul class="list-disc pl-6 space-y-3">
        <li>Apply at least the posted processing time plus a month before the trip, and longer around holidays or family events.</li>
        <li>Write a clear invitation letter with your address in B.C., your status in Canada, who is paying for what, and the planned dates.</li>
        <li>Show the visitor's own reasons to return: a job letter, business registration, property records or family at home.</li>
        <li>Check whether they qualify for an eTA before paying for a visa.</li>
        <li>Don't book non-refundable flights until the visa is in the passport.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Planning to bring family over permanently rather than for a visit? Read <a href="/articles/how-to-immigrate-to-canada-with-family" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">how to immigrate to Canada with your family</a> and our <a href="/articles/bc-pnp-spouse-family-guide-2026" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">BC PNP spouse and family guide</a>. If you are a temporary worker in B.C. thinking about your own path to permanent residence, see the <a href="/articles/canada-temporary-resident-cap-2027-bc-work-permit-holders" class="text-blue-600 hover:text-blue-800 underline">2027 temporary resident cap explainer</a> and estimate your score with the <a href="/bc-pnp-calculator" class="text-sky-700 underline font-semibold">BC PNP Calculator</a>.
      </p>

      <h2 id="faq" class="text-3xl font-bold mt-12 mb-6">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>

      <div class="space-y-6">
        {faqs.map((f) => (
          <div>
            <h3 class="text-xl font-semibold mb-2">{f.q}</h3>
            <p>{f.a}</p>
